Overview of `Zebra'
*******************

   Today TCP/IP network is covering all over the world.  The Internet
has deployed many countries and companies.  If you are connecting to the
Internet or using TCP/IP LAN network, you will need TCP/IP routing.

   If your box has more than two interfaces, it can works as a router.
OK, OK I know it can become a router without `GNU Zebra'.  Only thing
you have to do is setting up the interfaces and put a few command about
static route and/or default route.  It's enough to small network or stub
area.  But network is rather large or network structure changes
frequently, you will need dynamic routing protocol such as RIP or OPSF.
If you need dynamic routing, `GNU Zebra' is for you.

   `GNU Zebra' is a free software which provides `Distributed Routing
System'.  GNU Zebra is made from collection of some daemons.  Each
daemon has it's own terminal interface which called VTY, so you can
dynamically change the configuration and view routing table information.
It includes many protocol specific daemons.  `RIPd' handles RIP
protocol. `OSPFd' is off course a daemon which supports OSPF version 2
described in RFC2178.  `BGPd' daemon supports BGP-4 protocol described
in RFC1771 (A Border Gateway Protocal 4).  `zebra' is routing manager
and it does kernel routing table management.

   `zebra' is a central server which manages kernel routing table
updates and interface setup.  There are several protocol management
daemons: `ripd', `ripngd', `ospfd' and `bgpd'.

